# Virtual Pat — Quiet-by-Design at MWAA: What We Already Own, and What We Could Ship by Thursday

**Role in this run:** The engineer-tinkerer voice. My job is not to spec the ideal standard. My job is to keep the Strategist from concluding that a quiet-by-design standard requires a new capital line, a new vendor stack, and a five-year rollout — when most of the useful moves live inside assets MWAA already paid for and inside procedures MWAA already controls. The 20-billion-dollar Dulles program will bury this decision if we let it. The elegant version costs about 2 percent of what a comprehensive one would, and it ships before Concourse E opens in September 2026.

Below: the asset inventory read; five ordered solutions with cost, mechanism, resistance, and failure mode; precedents that already run each idea; the 90-day pilot tier; and the kill list.

---

## 1. Asset inventory read — what is already on the property

- **A PA system that is already zonable, and nobody is using the zones.** Every terminal in the MWAA system runs an IP-addressable public address plant tied into voice-evacuation infrastructure that must meet NFPA 72 Chapter 24 intelligibility for mass notification. Zoning is a licensing and configuration exercise, not a rewiring one. The reason IAD Concourse B sounds like a bus station is procedure, not equipment.
- **A FIDS network at both airports that already carries HDMI-class output to hundreds of screens.** Every one of those monitors can act as a locally-heard speaker for its own gate. The signal path exists. The audio channel is muted by convention.
- **Two major airline mobile apps (United at IAD, American at DCA) that already push gate change, boarding, and delay notifications to hundreds of thousands of paired boarding passes daily.** The redundant-visual channel the ADA-compliant standard is going to demand already reaches most passengers, in their pocket, in their preferred language, in vibrate mode. MWAA does not have to build it.
- **A 15-year airline use agreement in year one (2025–2039) with majority-in-interest and consultation provisions.** This is the strongest contract lever MWAA will hold for the next decade. Any acoustic, PA, and tenant paging obligation adopted now enters standard terms; adopted in 2033 it becomes an amendment fight.
- **A concourse under construction (IAD Concourse E, 435,000 sq ft, 14 gates, opening September 2026) and a decade of programmatic capital ahead of it under the July 29, 2026 Dulles transformation.** Standards baked into program briefs before schematic design cost roughly nothing. Standards baked in at commissioning cost 10–20x more. Standards imposed after CO cost 50–100x. The window is not "should we." It is "this quarter, or never at the same price."
- **Off-peak physical capacity that already exists in the buildings.** Mobile-lounge staging bays scheduled for retirement under the AeroTrain expansion, mezzanine areas at IAD's Main Terminal ticketing hall, closed retail bays waiting on concession turnover, the interior of the old East Building — floor plate that is already conditioned, already sprinkled, and already accessible. Any quiet-space program that starts with "we will build" instead of "we will repurpose" is leaving the cheap version on the table.

