## 2. Five solutions, ordered from most to least achievable

### Solution A — The gate-local announcement rule (SFO in a box)

**Problem it attacks:** Terminal-wide "final boarding" and "please do not leave your bag" broadcasts are the single largest source of ambient noise and the single largest source of passenger fatigue in the current MWAA acoustic environment. They also swamp the announcements that matter (irregular ops, security).

**Mechanism:** Reconfigure the existing PA zoning at both airports so that airline boarding, paging, and final-call announcements play only in the gate's boarding zone (nominally a 50-foot radius). Terminal-wide broadcast is reserved for TSA, security, irregular ops, and emergency communication. This is the same policy San Francisco International rolled out in 2018; SFO reports about 90 fewer minutes of announcements per day and a 40 percent reduction in boarding-area announcement volume.

**What it recombines:** Existing PA hardware. Existing zoning capacity. Existing airline gate-agent procedures. Nothing new.

**Cost:** Under $50,000 per airport — PA audit, zone re-mapping, updated airline standard operating procedures inside the use agreement, and a commissioning sweep of speech transmission index (STI) after re-zoning to prove the boarding-zone signal-to-noise is still ≥10 dB(A) and STI ≥0.45 (the ACRP Report 175 minimum for pier-style lounges).

**Implementation path:** 90 days. Board resolution not required — this fits within the CEO's Design Manual and Airport Operations Directive authority. Airline consultation needed but not consent, because it does not alter airline premises.

**Who resists and why:** Airline gate staff worried about missed-boarding metrics. Airline corporate communications teams whose paging scripts are centrally controlled. The mitigation is measurement — SFO's evidence is that missed-boarding rates did not measurably rise.

**Failure mode:** Poorly-tuned zones bleed audio into adjacent gates. Two adjacent boarding events with two different agents both talking at once produces intelligibility below the pre-change baseline. Commissioning must include an adjacent-zone spillover test at each gate cluster.

### Solution B — The announcement governance layer (make the invisible visible)

**Problem it attacks:** No one at MWAA — not operations, not communications, not the airlines — currently sees a live count of how many announcements were made in each terminal in the last hour, by whom, of what duration, at what dB level. You cannot regulate what you do not measure. Every "we should reduce announcements" conversation dies because nobody agrees on the current baseline.

**Mechanism:** Instrument the existing IP-based PA system to log every trigger event: origin (gate microphone, airline system, TSA, MWAA ops), duration, zone, timestamp. Feed the log to a public-facing operations dashboard visible to airport ops, airline duty managers, and the CEO's morning brief. Overlay against a target — say, 30 announcements per hour per concourse peak, 10 off-peak — and let the data become the referee.

**What it recombines:** The existing PA logging capability (already in modern IP-based airport PA systems from Bosch, TOA, Barix), the existing MWAA operational data lake, and the existing airline scorecard practice under the use agreement.

**Cost:** Under $150,000 for both airports — an internal data integration project. Zero new hardware. Zero new vendors if MWAA's existing enterprise reporting stack can host the dashboard.

**Implementation path:** 120 days to a working dashboard. Governance layer (targets, exceptions, escalations) authored inside the existing Airport Operations Directive.

**Who resists and why:** Airline duty managers who correctly perceive this as a benchmarking tool they cannot game. The counter is that the same tool tells them when they are being unfairly blamed (e.g., TSA is the biggest offender in the boarding area, not United).

**Failure mode:** Dashboard becomes theater. Nobody enforces the target. Kills itself in 18 months.

### Solution C — Beacon-triggered app notifications at the seat you are sitting in

**Problem it attacks:** The reason gate agents make loud terminal-wide "we are now boarding group 4" calls is that they do not trust that everyone in the hold room knows what group is boarding. The information is on the FIDS, but nobody is looking at it — they are looking at their phone. If we could deliver the message directly to the phone the passenger is already looking at, at the exact moment they are within 40 feet of the gate, the loud call becomes unnecessary.

**Mechanism:** Install a Bluetooth Low Energy (BLE) beacon at each gate (\$25–\$60 per beacon, battery-powered, PoE-optional). When a paired boarding pass in the United or American app enters the beacon's range, the app triggers a local push notification tied to that gate's current boarding status. Airline apps already handle the push and permission logic — MWAA is providing the beacon infrastructure and the standardized beacon-ID-to-gate map.

**What it recombines:** Existing airline apps. Existing passenger-owned phones. Existing gate structure. Existing airport-provided Wi-Fi and PoE. New device: a $40 beacon.

**Cost:** IAD has roughly 140 gates; DCA has 80. Full deployment: 220 beacons at \$60 each, plus \$50,000 in integration engineering with United and American. Total under \$75,000.

**Implementation path:** 180 days, contingent on airline app-team cooperation. United is a formal partner in the July 2026 Dulles announcement — this is precisely the kind of joint operational win the political framing supports.

**Who resists and why:** Passengers without the airline app (roughly 15–25 percent of leisure travelers). Passengers without smartphones (older adults, some international travelers). The redundant channels — FIDS, visual boarding-group indicator at the gate podium, and a staffed final call in the boarding zone — must remain mandatory and this must be documented, or the standard fails the ADA and DOT ACAA accessibility test.

**Failure mode:** Airline app adoption plateaus, or airlines decline to participate. Even at 60 percent adoption the ambient noise reduction is meaningful, so partial success is still success.

### Solution D — Parametric-array audio pods at gate seating clusters

**Problem it attacks:** When you must make a spoken announcement, the physics of a conventional cone loudspeaker say the sound goes everywhere within its coverage pattern. Everyone within 100 feet hears the "final call for Newark" even if they are boarding Los Angeles at the next gate.

**Mechanism:** Deploy Holosonics-class parametric-array (audio spotlight) speakers over each seating cluster in the boarding lounge. The speaker projects a narrow, ultrasonically-modulated audio beam that produces audible sound only where it intersects a listening surface (the seated passenger). Passengers in adjacent clusters — and passengers walking through the concourse — hear nothing. The technology is 20 years mature and in wide museum, retail, and healthcare deployment.

**What it recombines:** The existing announcement content, the existing gate seating layout, the existing ceiling plenum. New hardware.

**Cost:** Holosonics AS-16iX / AS-24iX-class units run roughly \$1,500–\$3,500 per speaker plus installation. Assume six speakers per gate cluster: \$15,000–\$25,000 per gate installed. Full IAD retrofit: \$2.1M–\$3.5M. Concourse E only (14 gates), integrated during construction: \$300K–\$500K. This is meaningfully more than solutions A–C, but it is still one-tenth of one percent of the \$22.5B Dulles program.

**Implementation path:** For Concourse E — this quarter, as a design-scope change to the fitout package, before millwork sets. For existing concourses — pilot at one IAD hold room in 2027, evaluate, then decide.

**Who resists and why:** The IT/AV vendor of record, who does not carry the line. Cabling and control-system integrators worried about a nonstandard product in their commissioning matrix. Fire marshals worried about NFPA 72 conflict (there isn't one — voice-evacuation stays on the primary system).

**Failure mode:** The beam requires a listening surface. Standing passengers hear less, or nothing. So this is a supplement to conventional zones, not a replacement.

### Solution E — The Concourse E "quiet pod" using existing off-peak space

**Problem it attacks:** Any quiet-by-design standard that reduces ambient PA output must produce a positive, staffed, low-stimulation alternative for passengers who need it — travelers with sensory-processing conditions, autism, PTSD, dementia, and the escorted-unaccompanied-minor population. Without that, "quiet-by-design" reads as "designed for able-bodied frequent flyers" and dies on the accessibility review.

**Mechanism:** Repurpose 1,000–1,500 sq ft of underused off-peak floor plate — a mobile-lounge staging area at IAD scheduled for retirement, a mezzanine adjacent to Concourse E, or a closed retail bay — as a staffed quiet pod. Curtained partitions, portable acoustic panels (NRC ≥0.85), dimmable lighting, no PA speakers, a Sunflower Lanyard station, and a supervising customer-experience host on the existing MWAA guest-services roster. Pittsburgh International's 1,500 sq ft sensory space runs on a similar model.

**What it recombines:** Existing floor plate. Existing HVAC. Existing guest-services staffing. Existing MWAA relationships with disability-community advisory groups (which every large-hub does — or should — convene).

**Cost:** \$30,000–\$80,000 in build cost plus \$120,000–\$200,000 per year in incremental host staffing per pod. Two pods (one IAD, one DCA) capitalize under \$200K.

**Implementation path:** 6 months to first pod at IAD Concourse E; 9 months to a DCA pod inside the North Concourse or the Terminal 2 connector.

**Who resists and why:** Concessions revenue team (opportunity cost of retail rent on the reclaimed space). Accessibility advocates (correctly) if the pod is used as an excuse to skip broader universal-design improvements. Airline duty managers who worry it becomes an unofficial employee break room.

**Failure mode:** Underused because passengers do not know it exists. Signage and airline-app integration must be part of the launch, not an afterthought.

