## 3. Precedents — operations that already run something like each of these

- **San Francisco International Airport, Quiet Airport Program (2018–present).** Ended terminal-wide "final call" pipes; boarding announcements confined to gate zone. Airport-reported reductions: ~90 fewer minutes of announcement audio per day; 40 percent reduction in boarding-area PA volume. Public-domain policy. This is Solution A, running for eight years, in a US Part 139 airport of comparable operational complexity. [Source: https://simpleflying.com/airport-quiet-policy-frustrating-passengers-sfo/] [Source: https://viewfromthewing.com/san-francisco-airport-removed-90-minutes-of-daily-noise-travelers-say-it-changed-everything/]
- **Amsterdam Airport Schiphol, silent airport policy (approximately 2011–present).** Nearly all non-emergency announcements eliminated in favor of FIDS, mobile-app push notifications, and staffed information desks. Documentation exists on Schiphol's own accessibility pages for how the deaf, blind, and cognitively-impaired passenger journey is handled without a PA-first assumption. Note: Schiphol's terminal geometry is more homogenous than IAD's, so direct transplant of policy without an accessibility-redundancy layer would fail at MWAA. [Source: https://news.schiphol.com/silence-at-amsterdam-airport-schiphol/] [Source: https://www.schiphol.nl/en/assistance/flying-for-the-deaf-and-blind/]
- **Pittsburgh International Airport, Presley's Place sensory room (2019).** 1,500 sq ft dedicated low-stimulation space including a replica cabin. Built with community partners at modest capital cost; operated inside existing airport-services envelope. Proof that Solution E scales, that a US mid-large hub can build one without a capital line, and that it does not become an unstaffed liability. [Source: https://www.npr.org/sections/health-shots/2019/08/08/746936601/kids-and-adults-with-autism-flying-easier-in-pittsburgh-with-airports-help]
- **NICU "HUSH" quiet-hour protocols (multiple US pediatric hospitals).** American Academy of Pediatrics recommends a maximum NICU noise level of ≤45 dB(A); measured NICU averages run 70–80 dB(A). Documented interventions that closed a large fraction of the gap were entirely procedural: designated 2-hour quiet blocks per shift, staff education across 22 disciplines, monitored alarm-volume standards, visual cues when noise exceeded target. This is the direct analog for Solution B — the policy-and-measurement layer that produces most of the gain before any capital hits the floor. [Source: https://publications.aap.org/pediatrics/article/144/2_MeetingAbstract/154/3396/Reducing-Noise-in-the-Neonatal-Intensive-Care-Unit] [Source: https://www.intechopen.com/chapters/1168747]
- **Walt Disney World and Universal Epic Universe quiet rooms + First Aid dual-use spaces.** Theme-park precedent for how a low-cost, dual-use quiet space is staffed, signposted, and integrated into an existing guest-services function. Relevant because the operational model — not the aesthetic — is the transferable asset. [Source: https://disneyworld.disney.go.com/guest-services/neurodivergent/] [Source: https://bethanygracetravels.com/2026/05/25/epic-universe-quiet-room-everything-sensory-sensitive-families-need-to-know/]

