## Closing note to the Strategist

The version of this standard that reads as a nine-figure capital program will not survive the July 29, 2026 Dulles-transformation cost pressure. It will get deferred, then studied, then quietly dropped in FY2028. The version that survives is a **tiered standard** in which Tier 1 — the mandatory floor — is almost entirely procedural, measurement-based, and drawn against assets MWAA already owns; Tier 2 is design-integrated into the Concourse E and future-concourse packages while the paint is still wet; and Tier 3 is genuinely capital-hungry work (acoustic ceilings, parametric arrays at scale) staged over the decade of the announced program.

The persuasive line in the Council report is not "MWAA should adopt a quiet-by-design standard." Every large hub says that. The persuasive line is: "here is the version of the standard that costs 2 percent of the comprehensive one, uses the PA plant, FIDS network, and airline apps we already own, ships three moves before Concourse E opens in September 2026, and generates the evidence base to defend or kill the expensive tiers on real MWAA data." That is the standard that gets adopted. Everything else is a slide deck.
